er; longer adjectives often use more. Introduce the second item with than. Avoid adding more to an -er comparative.","examples":["This route is shorter than that one.","The new seat is more comfortable."]},{"title":"Comparing a group","explanation":"Use the plus a superlative: often -est or most plus the adjective. Good becomes better/best; bad becomes worse/worst.","examples":["This is the safest path.","That was the best result."]},{"title":"Equal degree","explanation":"Use as plus the base adjective plus as for equality. Spelling may change in comparative and superlative forms.","examples":["The two boxes are as heavy as each other.","big → bigger → biggest"]}],"tips":["Read the full context before choosing.","Check the explanation, including after a correct answer.","A passing practice session is useful feedback, not a language certificate."],"source":"seed","provenance":{"version":"foundations-2026-09-v1","authorship":"Grammacho; generated offline with AI assistance","kind":"original-guidance-aligned","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending","referenceIds":["bc-comparisons","pcc-esol"],"assessment":"Practice only; levels are approximate teaching labels, not validated CEFR assessment."}}'::jsonb j) payload
